    name: Alchemical Guide
    shared_name: 
    status: 1
    blurb: >-
      # $[objectname]


      *"Vendors and traders provide easy access to tools, equipment, potions and poisons for those with enough funds to accommodate oftentimes inflated prices. However, the savvy adventurer knows that the world around them is rich with potential reagents, waiting only for someone with the right aptitude and inclination to harness them.*


      *Welcome, kindred seekers of wisdom and worth. With each turn of the page, we shall delve deep into the vibrant tapestry of minerals holding essence of earth, the tantalizing secrets of plants to both harm and heal, and the verdant bounty sealed beneath the flesh and form of the creatures roaming the wilds.*


      *Let our words be your compass, our knowledge serve as your guide, as we extract the treasures of the natural world together."*  

      \- ***The Lord's Harvest: Secrets of Nature's Bounty***, *by Leafwhisper Aelendir*


      <br>


      </br>


      ## Index


      - Part 1: Reagent Gathering
        - Sourcing and Searching
        - Harvesting
      - Part 2: Alchemy
        - The Basics
        - Potions
        - Poisons
        - Poultices
      - Part 3: Crafting
        - The Basics
        - Lirandelai: Alchemical Crafting
      - Part 4: @[](uncehewmmc)
        - @[](pfuakxdwta)
        - @[](fxwhwqwcgm)
        - @[](mktnnbueuc)
        - @[](paxgbkwryj)

      <br>


      </br>


      ## Part 1: Reagent Gathering


      Before you can brew a potion or forge a sword, you first need the materials used in their creation. This can be broken into three distinct steps: Determining what reagents you require. Finding those reagents out in the world. And harvesting them effectively so that they can be used. While only you can determine which reagents you wish to use (Perhaps with the help of the @[](uncehewmmc)), we will cover finding and harvesting your desired reagents within this chapter.


      <pre>


      </pre>



      ### Sourcing and Searching


      The savvy adventurer knows to gather and harvest whenever the opportunity arises; you never know when you might need an extra unit of Arthite! Throughout your travels you will encounter different reagents in the wild that require only the knowledge and skill to harvest them. This sub-section covers instead the instance where you require a specific reagent for your goals.  

      First you must identify where such a material can be found. This can be done through Downtime. 


      ***Resources.*** Typically, a character needs access to a library, hunter, geologist or herbalist (According to the reagent they are seeking). Assuming such access is available, this research requires one workweek of effort and at least 50ib spent on materials, bribes, gifts, and other expenses.


      ***Resolution.*** The character declares the specifics of their search—typically the location of a reagent or creature, but a character may seek a particular property instead. Note, learning what reagent houses what property and the location of that property are two different items to learn.  

      After one workweek, the character makes an Intelligence check with a +1 bonus per additional 100ib spent, up to a maximum bonus of +6. A character with access to a particularly well-stocked library or knowledgeable individual gains advantage on this check.  

      The character learns an amount of lore as shown by the details below.


      ##### Research Outcomes:

      - 1-5: Nothing

      - 6-10: 1 Piece of Lore

      - 11-20: 2 Pieces of Lore

      - 21+: 3 Pieces of Lore


      Once a character has identified the location of the reagent they wish to acquire, the character must then travel to the required destination. You may be further required to make a Wisdom (Survival) check to track a creature, or to locate the specific plant or mineral vein.


      <br>


      </br>


      ### Harvesting


      Your desired reagent is in your sights, whether following weeks of work to locate it, or happy accident! Now you need only successfully harvest it, which can prove a challenge in and of itself. Different reagents will require different hurdles to be overcome during this process. 


      #### Arcane


      ***Identification.*** A character must succeed on an Intelligence (Arcana) check to identify the means through which the reagent can be harvested. 


      ***Harvesting.***


      <br>


      </br>


      #### Fauna


      ***Identification.*** Some reagents, such as fur or scales, provide no difficult in identifying how to harvest them. For others a character must succeed on an Intelligence (Nature) check to identify the means through which the creature can be harvested. This check may be replaced by another for unusual creatures, such as an Arcana check for an Elemental, or a Religion check for a Fiend or Celestial. The DC is determined by the rarity of the creature, in addition to its CR. A successful check reveals all harvestable reagents from this creature.


      ***Immobilization*** Unlike most reagents, creatures will not simply stand still while you attempt to harvest them. Before you can harvest, the creature must be Incapacitated or Dead. Some Reagents may only be accessible from a creature while it remains alive.


      ***Harvesting.*** Once immobilized, a character may attempt to harvest reagents from a creature. To do so, choose which reagent you are harvesting from the @[Fauna Reagent](fxwhwqwcgm) list (Where applicable). You may choose a number of Reagents as determined by the size of the creature (See the *Creature Harvesting* table below), but must choose a different Reagent for each attempt.  

      *Each Reagent may be harvested a maximum of twice from a Gargantuan or Colossal creature.


      For each Reagent, make an Intelligence or Wisdom (Survival) check (**DC: 15 + 1/2 of the creature's CR**, rounded down, minimum of 0).  

      On each success, you gain a number of Units of Reagent equal to the number listed in the *Creature Harvesting* table below. A Critical Success (Natural 20) results in 1 additional Unit being harvested, while a Critical Failure (Natural 1) reduces the Max Check number by 1.


      ##### Creature Harvesting


      | Size        | Max Checks | Units Gained |

      |:-----------:|:----------:|:------------:|

      | Tiny        | 1          | 1/2          |

      | Small       | 1          | 1            |

      | Medium      | 2          | 1            |

      | Large       | 3          | 1            |

      | Huge        | 4          | 2            |

      | Gargantuan* | 6          | 2            |

      | Colossal*   | 8          | 3            |


      The harvest time required is detailed on each Reagent. Whenever you fail the check to harvest a Reagent by 5 or more, you suffer the Penalty detailed on that Reagent (Where applicable).


      <br>


      </br>


      #### Flora


      ***Identification.*** A character must succeed on an Intelligence (Nature) check to identify the means through which the desired reagent can be harvested. This check may be replaced by another for unusual flora, such as an Arcana check for an enchanted plant, or a Persuasion check for a sentient plant. The DC is typically 15, but may be adjusted by the rarity of the reagent.


      ***Harvesting.*** Flora typically do not require the character to make a check to harvest. However, more exotic or rarer plants may require a Dexterity (Sleight of Hand) check.


      After locating the desired reagent, make a Luck check to determine how many units are present. Consult the *Plant Harvest* table below. 


      | Luck    | Max Units |

      |:-------:|:---------:|

      | 1 - 5   | 0         |

      | 6 - 10  | 1         |

      | 11 - 15 | 1d4       |

      | 16 - 18 | 1d4+1     |

      | 19      | 1d4+2     |

      | 20      | 1d4+4     |

      | 21+     | 2d4+2     |


      Harvesting flora typically takes 20 minute per unit gathered.


      <br>


      </br>


      #### Mineral


      ***Identification.*** Minerals typically do not require the character to identify a means to harvest them, but may require an Intelligence (Nature) check to locate unexposed veins . For rarer or more volatile minerals, you may need to make an Intelligence (Nature) check to identify the means through which you can harvest the desired reagent.


      ***Harvesting.*** The character attempting to harvest from the mineral vein requires a Miner's Pick. You must make a Strength (Athletics) check. The DC for this check is typically 15. A result of less than the DC reduces the number of units extracted by an amount equal to the difference between the Check and the DC.  

      If the character has Tool Proficiency with Masonry Tools, they make this check with advantage when harvesting ores or stones.  

      If the character has Tool Proficiency with Jeweller's Tools, they make this check with advantage when harvesting gemstones. 


      The harvest time required is detailed on each Reagent. When you complete your attempt to harvest, you gain 1d4 units of Mineral.

      For check results 5 or more above the DC, you gain one additional unit for each 5 above the DC.


      <br>


      </br>


      <br>


      </br>


      ## Part 2: Alchemy


      *"Amidst swirling cauldrons and bubbling glassware, the art of Alchemy beckons. Here, within the realm of masters, lies secrets of source, soul and synthesis. Step into my den, allow your senses to be tantalized by the aromas of exotic herbs, rare components and bubbling concoctions. From healing draughts, to empowering brews, to brews more sinister.*


      *Welcome, aspiring Alchemical Masters, to the art of Alchemy."*  

      \- ***The Lord's Harvest: Secrets of Nature's Bounty***, *by Leafwhisper Aelendir*


      <br>


      </br>


      ### The Basics

      Once your shelves are stocked with your desired Reagents, you are ready to put them to use. Unfortunately, not all Reagents are alchemically useful, while those that are must be used in the correct manner to produce a successful creation. Alchemically reactive Reagents can be divided into three categories for the purpose of alchemy: Base, Catalyst, Ingredients. 


      A complete creation is made up of:

      - One Unit of Base Reagent

      - One Unit of Catalyst

      - One Unit or more of Ingredients


      This is true for all fields of Alchemy, whether Potion, Poison or Poultice. During crafting, a single Reagent cannot be used to fulfil two or more categories. e.g. You cannot use Water as a Reagent for both the Alchemical Base and Alchemical Catalyst for a creation, regardless of the number of units used.


      To perform any alchemical activity, a character requires access to the specific Artisan Tool in addition to having Proficiency with that tool relevant to the specific field they are working within: 

      - ***Potions.*** Alchemist's Supplies

      - ***Poisons.*** Poisoner's Kit

      - ***Poultices.*** Herbalism Kit


      <br>


      </br>


      #### Base

      The Base Reagent, typically a liquid, serves as the primary compound within which the other reagents will combine. Poor Base Reagents limit the maximum number of Ingredients that can be successfully incorporated, which also limits the maximum potential a alchemical creation can achieve. Consult the *Alchemy Base* table below.


      ##### Alchemy Base

      | Quality   | Max Ingredients | Example Reagent |

      |:---------:|:---------------:|:----------------|

      | Common    | 1               | @[](rlbskslmtb)

      | Uncommon  | 2               | Oil

      | Rare      | 3               | @[Primordial Water](wlinndxboq)

      | Very Rare | 5               | @[Spiritual Water](asvcqebtju)

      | Legendary | 7               | @[Essence of Divinity](lfvysylxrb)


      An alchemical creation's rarity cannot exceed the rarity of its Base, and the crafting process consumes a number of Units of Alchemical Base equal to the number of Ingredients used.


      <br>


      </br>


      #### Catalyst

      The catalyst serves as a means to unite the potentially divergent properties of the Ingredients into the final uniform result. The quality of Catalyst determines the maximum number of different properties a single alchemical creation can possess. Consult the *Alchemy Catalyst* table below. Additionally, higher quality Catalysts often impart additional effects to the creation unique to that Catalyst.


      ##### Alchemy Catalyst

      | Quality   | Max Properties  | Example Reagent |

      |:---------:|:---------------:|:----------------|

      | Common    | 0               |

      | Uncommon  | 1               |

      | Rare      | 2               |

      | Very Rare | 3               |

      | Legendary | 4               |


      Owing to it's purely additive feature, many Alchemists will choose to utilize a lower quality reagent for creations with a singular purpose: e.g. Alchemical fire, which seeks to only erupt into fire. The more experimental alchemist can produce creations with multiple simultaneous effects.


      While often difficult (and potentially expensive) to acquire, Catalysts are not consumed when used Alchemically for this purpose. All creations require 1 Unit of Alchemical Catalyst.


      <pre>


      </pre>


      ##### Catalyst and Base Compatibility


      Catalysts function more effectively when they are of the same Reagent type as is used in the Base. e.g. A Flora Catalyst will work complimentarily with a Flora Base.


      Catalysts that match the same Reagent Type as their base increase the Maximum Properties of the Alchemical Creation by 1.


      <br>


      </br>


      #### Ingredients

      The true art of Alchemy lies within the combination of Ingredients used and the subsequent effects they produce. There is a wide difference between throwing materials unknowingly into the pot, and carefully combining complimentary ingredients (Although one is absolutely more fun than the other).


      An Alchemical Creation is formed through the use of one or more Ingredients combining together. Ingredients can be divided into the two roles they serve:  

      ***Effect.***  Serving as the principle ingredient of the creation, this determines the type of potion produced.  

      ***Enhancement.*** Typically stabilising the alchemical creation, more potent varieties can further enhance the creation.



      All alchemically viable Ingredients can be divided into a number of subgroups depending on their composition; oils, salts, ashes, etc. 

      - Suspension

      - Salt

      - Essence

      - Ash

      - Vitriol (Metals)


      <br>


      </br> 


      #### Incompatible Reagents

      For each attempted creation, Potions, Poisons, Poultices, there are reagents that are incompatible with their production process. These vary between the fields of Alchemy requiring attention to detail for the avid brewer who would dabble in multiple fields. 


      An Incompatible Reagent cannot be used as part of the Alchemy process. Attempting to do so results in a failed creation, with all consumed reagents being lost in the process (This includes the Catalyst).


      <br>


      </br>


      ### Potions


      ***Tool Proficiency.*** Alchemist's Supplies


      ***Catalyst.*** Minerals


      ***Ingredients.*** Arcane, Animal Parts


      ***Incompatible.*** Flora


      <br>


      </br>


      ### Poisons


      ***Tool Proficiency.*** Poisoner's Kit


      ***Catalyst.*** Flora


      ***Ingredients.*** Arcane, Minerals


      ***Incompatible.*** Animal Parts


      <br>


      </br>


      ### Poultices


      ***Tool Proficiency.*** Herbalism Kit


      ***Catalyst.*** Animal Parts


      ***Ingredients.*** Arcane, Flora


      ***Incompatible.*** Minerals


      <br>


      </br>


      ## Part 3: Crafting


      *"The forging hammer sings its symphony as the birth of wonders, both magical and mundane, are brought to life. As you traverse the pages within, let your imagination ignite as you discover the diverse disciplines entombed within: from skilled blacksmiths forging mighty weapons to mystical weavers imbuing ethereal properties into woven wonders."*


      *Welcome, artisans and creators of every discipline, to the truest worship of the natural world itself."*  

      \- ***The Lord's Harvest: Secrets of Nature's Bounty***, *by Leafwhisper Aelendir*


      <br>


      </br>


      ### The Basics


      Whether supplied through hides of beasts and riches of the earth you've won yourself, or through the purchase of ores and leathers prepared for use, you find yourself ready to create your own equipment. All manner of items can be crafted when given reagents, time, and the necessary tool proficiency to see the job done. This part of the guide will cover the creation of Non-Magical items.


      First you must identify what you wish to create. To produce any Weapon, Armour, Artisanal Tool or piece of Adventuring Gear, you must have the appropriate Artisan's Tool and Proficiency before you can begin. Consult the below table for examples of what items can be created by Artisan Tools. 


      <pre>


      </pre>


      ##### Artisan Tool Examples

      | Artisan's Tool           | Weapons | Armour | Adventuring Gear | Artisan Tools |

      |:-------------------------|:-------:|:------:|:----------------:|:-------------:|

      | Brewer's Supplies        | — | — | Ale, Perfume, Wine | — |

      | Calligrapher's Supplies  | — | — | — | Playing card set, Three-dragon ante |

      | Carpenter's Tools        | — | — | Barrel, Ladder, *furniture* | — |

      | Cartographer's Tools     | — | — | *maps*| — |

      | Cobbler's Tools          | — | — | Climbing shoes, Snow shoes | — |

      | Cook's Utensils          | — | — | Banquet, Meat, Soap | — |

      | Glassblower's Tools      | Blowgun Needles, Dart | — | *glassware* | — |

      | Jeweller's Tools         | — | — | Holy Symbol (Amulet), Mirror (Steel), Signet Ring | — |

      | Leatherworker's Tools    | Whip | Light Armour, Hide | Backpack, Pouch, Quiver | — |

      | Mason's Tools            | — | — | Block and Tackle, Ram (Portable) | — |

      | Painter's Supplies       | — | — | Holy Symbol (Emblem), *art* | — |

      | Potter's Tools           | — | — | Flask, Holy Symbol (Reliquary) | — |

      | Smith's Tools            | Bolts, Battleaxe, Longsword | Chain Shirt, Heavy Armour, Shields | Ball Bearings, Lamp, Manacles, *metal objects* | — |

      | Tinker's Tools           | — | — | Chalk, Scale (Merchant), Whetstone | Artisan Tools |

      | Weaver's Tools           | — | Padded | Basket, Clothes, Sack, Tent, *cloth* | — |

      | Woodcarver's Tools       | Arrows, Club, Spear, Longbow | Shield, Wooden Armour | Pole (10ft) | Dice set, Flute, Pan Flute |


      Many more items can be produced other than those listed in the above table, and you may be able to make use of a different Artisan Tool to perform the same task as another. Consult your DM if you have any questions.


      <br>


      </br>


      #### Units for Crafting

      Different items require a different amount of reagent to be able to create them. This is typically determined based on the size of the item. Consult the table below.  

      If you are instead purchasing materials to produce a standard item, you require an amount of raw materials equal to half the item's selling cost.



      ##### Units for Crafting

      | Type of Item                           | Tiny |Small/Medium | Large | Huge | Gargantuan |

      |:---------------------------------------|:----:|:-----------:|:-----:|:----:|:----------:|

      | Ammunition or Simple Weapon            | 1    | 1           | 2     | 4    | 8

      | Martial Weapons or Light/Medium Armour | 1    | 2           | 4     | 8    | 16

      | Heavy Armour                           | 2    | 3           | 6     | 12   | 24

      | Barding                                | 3    | 4           | 8     | 16   | 32


      <br>


      </br>


      #### Crafting Duration

      The total time required to create an object is determined by the cost of the Reagents or materials used to create it. You can determine this cost in two ways:

      - Add together the total cost of all Reagents used in the crafting of this item

      - Consult the selling cost of the item.


      The total time required to create the object is the greater of these two values. This represents the material cost of the item, as well as the difficulty to forge such an item due to exotic materials or a complicated design.


      <br>


      </br>


      #### Crafting Progress


      A character progresses at a rate of 25ib per day spent crafting with access to Reagents, Tools, and the appropriate facilities (if necessary). A character can complete multiple items in a day if the item's combined cost is 25ib or lower. Items that cost more than 25ib can be completed over longer periods of time, as long as the work in progress is stored in a safe location. 


      Multiple characters can combine their efforts if they each possess the appropriate Artisan Tool and Proficiency. Divide the time needed to create an item by the number of characters working on it. The DM may determine a limited number of characters may work on a single item at once. 


      ##### Tiny Crafting and Exceptions


      While crafting particularly small objects do not consume many Reagents, they do require additional time to create. This may also be true of other particularly delicate objects as determined by your DM.  

      Crafting these items progresses more slowly as slow, at a rate of 10ib every day. This penalty may be removed through @[other means](cjentiuofg).


      <br>


      </br>


      ### Lirandelai: Alchemical Crafting

      *Pronounced: Lee-rran-de-lie*


      While many artisans and craftsmen can make an adequate—if not comfortable—life for themselves utilising the readily available materials harvested throughout Taltagor, those that seek perfection in their craft look beyond the mundane. Prevalent within Elvish traditions, Lirandelai (Translated: Nature's Blessing) is the practice of manipulating reagents to extract the fullest of their nature from within. While most smiths could theoretically forge a blade from Darksteel, only a master can allow the metal's intrinsic traits to electrify its attacks while leaving its wielder unharmed.


      <br>


      </br>


      #### Progress and Complications


      When attempting to craft an item using a Reagent of Uncommon rarity or higher, a character's skill determines how effective they are at producing that item. For every 100ib worth of progress that is made during the crafting of an item, the character involved must make a Skill Check using the appropriate Tool and Ability Score for the task (See the *Artisan Tool Checks* table below for general examples). A character with Proficiency in the appropriate Skill for the Tool they are using makes that check with advantage.  

      If multiple characters are working together to produce an item, a single check is made per 100ib progress by the character with the highest modifier amongst those contributing.


      ##### Artisan Tool Checks

      | Artisan's Tool           | Check                          |

      |:-------------------------|:------------------------------:|

      | Brewer's Supplies        | Constitution (Insight)         |

      | Calligrapher's Supplies  | Dexterity (History)            |

      | Carpenter's Tools        | Intelligence (Survival)        |

      | Cartographer's Tools     | Wisdom (Investigation)         |

      | Cobbler's Tools          | Intelligence (Insight)         |

      | Cook's Utensils          | Wisdom (Nature)                |

      | Glassblower's Tools      | Dexterity (Athletics)          |

      | Jeweller's Tools         | Intelligence (Sleight of Hand) |

      | Leatherworker's Tools    | Constitution (Survival)        |

      | Mason's Tools            | Strength (Investigation)       |

      | Painter's Supplies       | Charisma (Sleight of Hand)     |

      | Potter's Tools           | Dexterity (Performance)        |

      | Smith's Tools            | Constitution (Athletics)       |

      | Tinker's Tools           | Intelligence (Sleight of Hand) |

      | Weaver's Tools           | Wisdom (Investigation)         |

      | Woodcarver's Tools       | Dexterity (Survival)           |


      If an item's value is 100ib or less to produce, you make one Check at the end of the crafting process. Consult the *Lirandelai Outcomes* table below for the result of that Skill Check.  


      <br>


      </br>


      ##### Lirandelai Outcomes

      | Uncommon Item | Rare Item | Very Rare Item | Legendary Item | Outcome |

      |:-------------:|:---------:|:--------------:|:--------------:|:--------|

      | 1 - 5         | 1 - 5     | 1 - 10         | 1 - 10         | You suffer a significent setback. Roll twice on the *@[complications](jvbbwcjmss)* table.

      | 6 - 10        | 6 - 10    | 11 - 15        | 11 - 15        | You suffer a setback. Roll once on the *@[complications](jvbbwcjmss)* table. In addition, you are set back 50ib value of crafting time.

      | 11 - 15       | 11 - 20   | 16 - 20        | 16 - 24        | Crafting is proceeding as hoped.

      | 16 - 20       | 21 - 25   | 21 - 25        | 25 - 30        | Crafting is proceeding quickly. You progress an additional 25ib value of crafting time.

      | 21+           | 26+       | 26+            | 31+            | You make a breakthrough! You progress an additional 50ib value of crafting time.
